---
/**
 * The games hub, recomposed for the dawn pass (2026-09-21).
 *
 * What stood here: one composed card for the first game, the second game dropped underneath
 * as a plain stripe card a tenth the size, and then a charcoal block explaining how the
 * lines were gathered. Two games of equal standing shown as a headliner and a footnote, and
 * a page whose job is to get someone playing ending on process talk.
 *
 * Now both games get the same room: a dark card carrying its own mark, its own ink, the
 * counted facts and a small drawing of what a player actually meets. Play is in the band and
 * again on every card. Where the lines come from is said once, in the band's own line, and
 * in full after every answer inside the game.
 *
 * Every number on this page is read from the built game's metadata; nothing is typed but the
 * rules a game states on its own page (a run is ten lines, about two minutes).
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import GameCard from '../components/GameCard.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import type { IconName } from '../lib/icons';
import { liveGames } from '../lib/games';
import slsMeta from '../games/sounds-like-scripture.meta.json';
import wsiMeta from '../games/who-said-it.meta.json';

const games = liveGames();

/**
 * What each game shows on its card. The mark and the ink keep two dark cards from reading
 * as one; `run` is the length of a run, which each game states on its own page; the facts
 * are counted off the built pool; and `preview` is the drawing, whose line and names come
 * off that same metadata so a card is one real question out of the game and nothing typed.
 */
type CardSpec = {
  icon: IconName;
  ink: 'orange' | 'blue';
  run: number;
  /** The size of this game's pool, for the band's count across every game. */
  items: number;
  facts: string[];
  preview:
    | { kind: 'binary'; answers: [string, string]; line?: string | null }
    | { kind: 'names'; names?: string[] | null; line?: string | null };
};

const SPEC: Record<string, CardSpec> = {
  'sounds-like-scripture': {
    icon: 'scroll',
    ink: 'orange',
    run: 10,
    items: slsMeta.items,
    /* Short enough to stand on one row of pills at 390: three long ones stacked into three
       rows and pushed the drawing off the screen. */
    facts: [`${slsMeta.items} real lines`, `${slsMeta.sources} editions`],
    preview: {
      kind: 'binary',
      answers: ['Not in the Bible', 'In the Bible'],
      // One line from the built pool, word for word, with no citation beside it.
      line: (slsMeta as { cover?: string | null }).cover ?? null
    }
  },
  'who-said-it': {
    icon: 'quote',
    ink: 'blue',
    run: 10,
    items: wsiMeta.items || 0,
    facts: [
      ...(wsiMeta.items ? [`${wsiMeta.items} real lines`] : []),
      ...(wsiMeta.speakers ? [`${wsiMeta.speakers} speakers`] : [])
    ],
    /*
     * One real line and the four real names under it, both straight out of the built
     * pool's metadata, which never records which of the four spoke. Alphabetical, and
     * none of them marked: the card asks exactly what the first screen of the game asks.
     */
    preview: {
      kind: 'names',
      line: (wsiMeta as { cover?: string | null }).cover ?? null,
      names: (wsiMeta as { coverNames?: string[] | null }).coverNames ?? null
    }
  }
};

/**
 * WHAT A PLAYER IS ABOUT TO MEET, in words, under the cards.
 *
 * This page carried 175 words, which made the two games the thinnest pages on a site whose
 * whole hope is being found by someone searching for exactly this. The words below are the
 * ones a person wants before they tap Play: what the game asks, how long a run is, where the
 * lines come from, and what decides the answer. Not how any of it was made.
 *
 * Nothing here repeats a number the card above already prints, and nothing here is typed that
 * the game does not say on its own first screen ("Eight to sixteen seconds each") or in its
 * own footer (the editions, the canon). The works named in the second paragraph are the
 * sources in the built pool, in the order the pool lists them.
 */
type Say = { kicker: string; title: string; paras: string[] };

const SAY: Record<string, Say> = {
  'sounds-like-scripture': {
    kicker: 'Sounds Like Scripture',
    title: 'Is this line in the Bible?',
    paras: [
      'A line comes up on a card and you call it: in the Bible, or only sounds like it. Ten ' +
        'lines a run, eight to sixteen seconds on each, so a run is done inside a couple of ' +
        'minutes. Points go to speed, and a run of right answers is worth more.',
      /* Two hard spaces: a line that breaks after "1" leaves a bare numeral at the end of a
         column, and one that breaks after "à" leaves a bare accent. */
      /* "among them", because the pool holds eleven prose works and this names six of them:
         a colon list here would read as the whole shelf. */
      'The lines that are not Scripture are not inventions. They are sentences out of the ' +
        'Apocrypha and 1 Enoch, and out of books Christians have read for centuries, ' +
        'among them the Apostolic Fathers, Augustine, Thomas à Kempis, Julian of ' +
        'Norwich, John Bunyan and Josephus. Old English and plain modern English appear on ' +
        'both sides, so the way a line sounds gives nothing away.',
      'You choose the Bible you read before you start, Protestant, Catholic or Orthodox, and ' +
        'what counts as Scripture follows that choice. A book yours prints and another leaves ' +
        'out is never called a fake; the answer tells you where it is printed.'
    ]
  },
  'who-said-it': {
    kicker: 'Who Said It?',
    title: 'Which of these four spoke?',
    paras: [
      'A line of speech comes up with four names under it, and you tap the one who said it. ' +
        'Ten lines a run, ten to eighteen seconds on each, and the names you are given are ' +
        'people who could plausibly have been in the room.',
      'That is what makes it hard. Job’s friends sound like God, and the Pharisees sound like ' +
        'the disciples. No name is ever offered as the wrong answer when it could truthfully ' +
        'have said the line, so God is never set against Jesus, and the disciples are never ' +
        'set against Peter.',
      'Every line is speech the Bible records, from the sixty-six books, in the King James ' +
        'Version and the World English Bible, British Edition. As soon as you answer you are ' +
        'told who spoke and where to read it.'
    ]
  }
};

/* Every live game gets a room, drawn or not: a game whose drawing nobody has made yet still
   belongs on the hub, and the card simply carries its words and its way in. */
const specs = games.map(g => SPEC[g.slug]);
/** Counted across the whole shelf, so the band never reports one game's pool as the site's. */
const totalLines = specs.reduce((n, s) => n + (s?.items ?? 0), 0);
const runs = specs.filter(Boolean).map(s => s!.run);
const sameRun = runs.length > 0 && runs.every(r => r === runs[0]);

const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Games', url: '/games/' }];

/*
 * The hub was the one page of the four with no page-level markup at all, so it said nothing
 * about what kind of page it is while /quizzes/ and /articles/ both did. A list of the games,
 * in the order they are drawn, built off the same registry the cards are. No rich result comes
 * of it; it makes the page's type explicit and brings it in line with its siblings.
 */
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'Games',
  url: new URL('/games/', Astro.site).href,
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://wiserwalk.com/#website' },
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'ItemList',
    numberOfItems: games.length,
    itemListElement: games.map((g, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: g.title,
      url: new URL(g.href, Astro.site).href
    }))
  }
};
---
<Base
  title="Games"
  description="Games played against the clock that count what you know, never how devout you are. Every line and every answer is real and citable."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  {/*
    No accent on the title. Blue means the left pole of an axis everywhere else on the
    site, and a one-word page title painted entirely in it makes the accent mean nothing.
  */}
  <Hero
    title="Games"
    eyebrow="Against the clock"
    icon="scroll"
    kind="night"
    image="/img/game-manuscript.jpg"
    imageAlt=""
    imagePosition="50% 24%"
    tiles={[
      { n: games.length, label: games.length === 1 ? 'Game' : 'Games' },
      ...(totalLines ? [{ n: totalLines, label: 'Real lines' }] : []),
      ...(sameRun ? [{ n: runs[0]!, label: 'Lines a run' }] : [])
    ]}
  >
    Short, hard, and honest about their sources. A game here counts what you know, never how
    devout you are, and every line is quoted from a real edition.
    {games[0] && (
      <a slot="actions" class="btn" href={games[0].href}>
        Play {games[0].title} <Icon name="arrow" size={15} />
      </a>
    )}
  </Hero>

  <div class="ghub">
    {games.map((g, i) => {
      const s = SPEC[g.slug];
      return (
        <GameCard
          game={g}
          icon={s?.icon ?? g.icon}
          ink={s?.ink ?? (i % 2 === 0 ? 'orange' : 'blue')}
          clock={s ? '2 min a run' : undefined}
          facts={s?.facts}
          preview={s?.preview}
        />
      );
    })}
  </div>

  {/*
    The words under the cards. A card says what a game is in a sentence and shows one real
    question; this says what a player actually wants to know next, which is how a run goes,
    where the lines come from and what decides the answer. It is not a second card: plain
    panels, the site's own, one to a game, and one quiet line under both.
  */}
  <SectionHead title="How the games work" kicker="Before you play" />
  <div class="stack stack--2">
    {games.map(g => {
      const s = SAY[g.slug];
      return s ? (
        <section class="pnl">
          <div class="pnl-head">
            <p class="pnl-kicker">{s.kicker}</p>
            <h3 class="pnl-title">{s.title}</h3>
          </div>
          {s.paras.map(p => <p>{p}</p>)}
          <p class="gsay-go"><a href={g.href}>Play {g.title} <Icon name="arrow" size={14} /></a></p>
        </section>
      ) : null;
    })}
  </div>
  <p class="gsay-foot">
    Both are free, and there is nothing to sign up for. Every line in either game is quoted
    word for word from a public-domain edition, and the citation is on the screen as soon as
    you answer. None of them is one of the famous sayings people wrongly believe are in the
    Bible: the lines that catch players out are ordinary ones.
  </p>
</Base>
